<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Homer</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 8th century BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/8th-century-bce">8th century BCE</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Homer is the ancient Greek poet to whom the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iliad">Iliad</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/odyssey">Odyssey</a> are attributed, epic poems probably composed in the 8th or 7th century BCE from a long oral tradition. The Iliad tells of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trojan-war">Trojan War</a> and the Odyssey of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/odysseus">Odysseus</a>'s return home. Nothing certain is known of his life, but his poems were the foundation of Greek education and literature.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/homer.jpg" alt="Homer" width="320" height="427" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Photograph by Mike Peel (www.mikepeel.net). · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Homer_At_the_British_Museum_2024_(3x4_cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>The identity of Homer is a mystery, and scholars generally regard the ancient picture of a single author behind the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iliad">Iliad</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/odyssey">Odyssey</a> as a fictional narrative. Their composition is thought to date to the late eighth or early seventh century BCE. Many accounts of his life circulated in antiquity. The most widespread made him a blind bard from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ionia">Ionia</a>, a region including the central west coast of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anatolia">Anatolia</a> and the islands of Chios and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samos">Samos</a>, but modern scholars regard these accounts as apocryphal. The earliest implicit references to Homer, in the 7th century BCE, are in works by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/archilochus">Archilochus</a>, Alcman, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tyrtaeus">Tyrtaeus</a> and Callinus.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pindar">Pindar</a> is credited with saying that Homer came from Chios and Smyrna. The historian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ephorus">Ephorus</a> held that he studied poetry with the bard Phemius and was born in Cyme, Philochorus thought <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/argo">Argos</a> was his birthplace, and later writers believed it was Pylos or <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/athens">Athens</a>. The Byzantine Suda described him as descended from a Muse, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/apollo">Apollo</a>, Orpheus or others. No account of his origin became mainstream, but most place his death on the island of Ios, where two epigrams by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alcaeus">Alcaeus</a> of Messene commemorate his burial.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Works and ideas</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iliad">Iliad</a> centres on a quarrel between King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/agamemnon">Agamemnon</a> and the warrior <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/achilles">Achilles</a> in the last year of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trojan-war">Trojan War</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/odyssey">Odyssey</a> chronicles the ten-year journey home of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/odysseus">Odysseus</a>, king of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ithaca">Ithaca</a>, after the fall of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/troy">Troy</a>. Both are in Homeric Greek, a literary language mixing Ionic and Aeolic features, and are composed in unrhymed dactylic hexameters. Most researchers believe they were first transmitted orally, and set phrases such as crafty Odysseus and rosy-fingered Dawn aided the bard. Both begin with an invocation to the Muse.</p><p>The poems were put into written form between the eighth and sixth centuries BCE. Ancient tradition held that Pisistratus collected them in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/athens">Athens</a> in the late sixth century BCE. After the 24 books were fixed, scholars at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexandria">Alexandria</a> such as Aristarchus of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samothrace">Samothrace</a> helped establish a canonical text.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>The Homeric poems were widely used as school texts in Greek and Hellenistic cultures, the first literary works taught to all students. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/plato">Plato</a> said that Homer had educated <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greece">Greece</a>. Interpreters, especially the Stoics, read the poems as allegories containing hidden wisdom. The first printed edition was produced in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/florence">Florence</a> in 1488 by Demetrios Chalkokondyles. In 1873 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/heinrich-schliemann">Heinrich Schliemann</a> announced the discovery of Homer's Troy at Hisarlik in modern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turkey">Turkey</a>.</p><p>Ancient chronology dated the sack of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/troy">Troy</a> to 1184 BCE, and some scholars think the destruction of Troy VIIa around 1220 BCE gave rise to the myth. The poems depict customs from different periods: their heroes use bronze weapons but are cremated, an <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iron-age">Iron Age</a> practice. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/virgil">Virgil</a>'s <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aeneid">Aeneid</a> was heavily influenced by Homer, and later works from Tennyson's Ulysses to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/james-joyce">James Joyce</a>'s Ulysses retell his stories.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Homer” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The city produced notable scholars and engineers: Aristophanes of Byzantium, head of the Library of Alexandria around 200 BCE, edited <b>Homer</b> and other poets and is credited with developing the system of Greek accents, while Philo of Byzantium wrote on mechanics and siegecraft.</blockquote><a href="/empire/byzantium">Byzantium · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>Herodotus credits Glaucus of Chios with inventing the welding of iron, a family of sculptors descended from Archermus worked there in the sixth century BCE, and the Homeridae, a guild of rhapsodes who claimed descent from <b>Homer</b>, kept the island's claim to be his birthplace.</blockquote><a href="/empire/chios">Chios · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>Its people spoke Lydian, an Indo-European language of the Anatolian family related to Hittite and Luwian, and <b>Homer</b> knew the region as Maeonia.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-lydia">Kingdom of Lydia · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Homer</b> praised the skill of its craftsmen in glass and purple dye and the embroidery of its women.</blockquote><a href="/empire/sidon">Sidon · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>The region had been important in the Mycenaean age: a palace at Ayios Vasileios and a mansion at the Menelaion near the later city date from the Late Bronze Age, and <b>Homer</b> made Lacedaemon the kingdom of Menelaus and Helen.</blockquote><a href="/empire/sparta">Sparta · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>The historian Herodotus wrote, in the 5th century BCE, that <b>Homer</b> and Hesiod, some four hundred years before his own time, had taught the Greeks the genealogy of the gods, given them their titles and described their appearance.</blockquote><a href="/religion/ancient-greek-religion">Ancient Greek religion · Origins</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Died in Ios. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=36.72,25.32&amp;year=-735" aria-label="Ios on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5186 840 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the place of death of Homer"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5636 1110h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5636 1110h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the place of death. <a href="/atlas?at=36.72,25.32&amp;year=-735">What was here in 735 BCE? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
<section class="enc-words" id="in-their-words"><h2 class="enc-section-title">In their words</h2><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;Men come and go as leaves year by year upon the trees. Those of autumn the wind sheds upon the ground, but when spring returns the forest buds forth with fresh vines.&#8221;</p><footer>Iliad, book 6 &#183; tr. Samuel Butler, 1898 &#183; spoken by Glaucus to Diomedes &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/2199/pg2199-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;There is one omen, and one only—that a man should fight for his country.&#8221;</p><footer>Iliad, book 12 &#183; tr. Samuel Butler, 1898 &#183; spoken by Hector &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/2199/pg2199-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;I would rather be a paid servant in a poor man’s house and be above ground than king of kings among the dead.&#8221;</p><footer>Odyssey, book 11 &#183; tr. Samuel Butler, 1900 &#183; spoken by the shade of Achilles in the underworld &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/1727/pg1727-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><p class="enc-article-foot"><a class="enc-random-link" href="/encyclopedia/quotations">Quotations from 595 people &rarr;</a></p></section>
